  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure centered over the Ontario/Quebec border will progress \neast/southeast into New England as a shortwave trough moves in from \nthe central Plains into the Great Lakes. This combination will drive \na warm front across Michigan tomorrow with low-level flow veering \nfrom southeast to southwest in the wake of the front. This will usher\nin warmer temperatures tomorrow and Thursday with forecasted highs \npeaking in the 70s. For this evening, stable conditions maintain dry \nweather under broken mid to high level cloud cover. Overnight, there \nis a weak to moderate signal for shallow fog or very low-lying \nstratus focused on a convergent boundary as the aforementioned winds \nstart veering. Any fog/stratus would be most prominent around or \nnear Ann Arbor and into the urban Metro and north Metro region, \nfactoring in shallow moisture advection from Lake Erie and Lake St. \nClair. The one caveat for fog development will be the arrival and \nthickening of mid-level cloud cover ahead of the approaching \nshortwave. Some sprinkles or a weak rain shower cannot be ruled out \nwith the arrival of this wave. Some secondary fog chances will be \npossible across the Tri-Cities, coinciding with a brief period of \nmore effective radiational cooling as the trailing edge of the mid- \nlevel cloud shield exits, but this will trend very close to daybreak.\nAny fog or shallow stratus will rapidly erode after daybreak under \nthe established southwest flow. \n\nThe next chance for rain showers will enter Thursday afternoon and \nevening as a cold front progresses across SE MI. There will be a \nribbon of enhanced theta-e ahead of the front bringing precipitation\nchance for all locations. The better moisture is confined to 925 to \n750mb and overall forcing remains weak. This will limit coverage and \nwill produce weak qpf output for any showers observed, with totals \nranging between trace amounts up to several hundredths of an inch\nwith any rain observed. Otherwise, breezy conditions just ahead and \nin the wake of the front with gusts around 25 to 30 mph expected. \nPassage of the front will moderate temperatures slightly to end the \nweek but above normal conditions are still expected with highs in the\nlow to mid 60s. Above normal temperatures continue through the \nweekend and into early next week.",
